INT. RURAL RADIO STATION - NIGHT

A tin-roofed shack by a cornfield. Foam-padded walls. A glowing "ON AIR" sign. A single microphone on a boom.

LORENZO BIANCHINI, 50s, in a bathrobe and headphones, leans back in a swivel chair and sips coffee. Across the booth, FAYE OSTROWSKI, 20s, the board operator, scrolls through requests on a laptop. IBRAHIM SESAY, 40s, a truck driver, sits on a stool by the door, waiting out the rain. GRETA HALLSTROM, 60s, the station owner, knits in the corner.

LORENZO
(into mic)
It's two in the morning, and you're listening to Night Owl Radio. The rain is steady, the corn is quiet, and the phone lines are open.

FAYE
(whispering)
You're good. Five more minutes and we can close.

IBRAHIM
Can I stay until the rain stops?

GRETA
(not looking up)
You can stay until the pot runs out.

The phone line rings. Faye flips a switch.

FAYE
Night Owl, you're on the air.

MARCELINE (V.O.)
(over speaker, small, calm)
Hello, Lorenzo. It's Marceline.

Lorenzo's cup stops halfway to his mouth. Greta's knitting needles go still.

LORENZO
Marceline. Hello.

MARCELINE (V.O.)
I wanted to say thank you for the song last week.

LORENZO
You're welcome.

MARCELINE (V.O.)
I'm calling from the field behind the station. The corn is very tall tonight.

Faye looks at the window. Ibrahim stands. Beyond the glass, rain and darkness and the black wall of corn.

FAYE
(to Lorenzo, low)
That line is internal. There's no one out there.

MARCELINE (V.O.)
Lorenzo, would you play it again?

Lorenzo sets down the cup. He looks at Greta. She nods, ashen.

LORENZO
Of course, Marceline. Anything for you.

He cues a record. The needle drops. Outside, something taps gently on the glass.
